---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Tldr, Eli5, WhyItMatters, HowItWorks, FailureModes, WhatILearned, DeepDive } from '../components/blocks';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How LLMs Predict Text"
  description="A large language model doesn't look up answers. It predicts the next word. Here's a real, tiny version of that trick, running in your browser."
>
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/ai/">← AI</a></p>
  <h1>How LLMs Predict Text</h1>

  <Tldr>
    An LLM doesn't know facts the way a database does — it has no lookup
    table of "true things." What it actually does, at every single step,
    is predict the <strong>most statistically likely next word</strong>
    given everything written so far, based on patterns learned from a huge
    amount of text. There's no separate "understanding" module —
    prediction is the whole mechanism. Scale that simple trick up with
    enough data and compute, and it starts producing essays, code, and
    conversation that read as if something understood the question.
  </Tldr>

  <Eli5>
    You've finished a friend's sentence before — not by reading their
    mind, but because you've heard them talk a thousand times and picked
    up the pattern. Now imagine doing that, but:
    <ul>
      <li>you'd "listened" to a meaningful slice of everything ever written, not just one friend</li>
      <li>you could recall patterns from millions of conversations at once</li>
      <li>you did the guessing in a fraction of a second, one word at a time, for as long as needed</li>
    </ul>
    That's an LLM — the exact same trick, guess the next word from
    patterns, done at a scale no single human could match. It's also why
    it can sound completely confident while being completely wrong:
    confidence and correctness were never the same thing when the entire
    mechanism is a guess.
  </Eli5>

  <WhyItMatters>
    <ul>
      <li>This is why LLMs sound confident even when they're <strong>wrong</strong> — fluent-sounding text and true text are produced by the same mechanism.</li>
      <li>It's why more context in your prompt changes the output — you're changing what the model conditions its guess on.</li>
      <li>It's why the same question worded differently can get a different answer — different words, different prediction.</li>
    </ul>
  </WhyItMatters>

  <HowItWorks>
    <p>Two things decide how good the prediction is:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Context window</strong> — how much prior text the model looks back at.</li>
      <li><strong>Training data + model size</strong> — how nuanced the learned patterns are.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The lab below uses a context window of exactly <strong>one word</strong>
      and a training set of a few hundred words. Real models use context
      windows of thousands to hundreds of thousands of tokens, trained on
      a meaningful fraction of all text on the internet. Same idea, wildly different scale.
    </p>
  </HowItWorks>

  <section class="lab" aria-labelledby="lab-heading">
    <h2 id="lab-heading" class="eyebrow section-heading">Try it yourself: a real (tiny) predictor</h2>
    <p class="lab-note">
      Runs entirely in your browser. This model was trained — just now, on
      your machine — on a ~230-word toy story about a cat and a dog. That's
      the whole "model." Everything below is real math on real (tiny) data,
      not a canned demo.
    </p>

    <div class="corpus-stats" id="corpus-stats" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">Training words</span><span class="cs-val" id="stat-total">—</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">Vocabulary</span><span class="cs-val" id="stat-vocab">—</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">Learned contexts</span><span class="cs-val" id="stat-contexts">—</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">Context window</span><span class="cs-val">1 word</span></div>
    </div>

    <label for="seed-input" class="pw-label">Type a word or short phrase (try "the cat", "the dog", "the mailman"):</label>
    <input id="seed-input" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="the cat" value="the cat" />

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="predict-table" id="predict-table" aria-live="polite">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Next word candidate</th><th>Seen this often</th><th>Model's confidence</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="predict-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="generate-row">
      <button type="button" id="generate-btn" class="ex-btn">Generate 15 more words →</button>
    </div>
    <p class="generated-output" id="generated-output" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </section>

  <FailureModes>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"It looked that up."</strong> It didn't. It predicted plausible-sounding text. Sometimes plausible and true line up; sometimes they don't.</li>
      <li><strong>"It's just autocomplete, so it's not impressive."</strong> The mechanism is genuinely that simple. What's impressive is what that mechanism can do at a large enough scale — the toy above literally cannot hold a coherent thought past a few words; a real model can, from the same core idea plus orders of magnitude more scale.</li>
      <li><strong>"It sounded confident, so it's probably right."</strong> Confidence and correctness come from unrelated places in this architecture. Fluency is cheap; verify anything that matters.</li>
    </ul>
  </FailureModes>

  <DeepDive>
    <p>
      Real models differ from this toy in three specific ways — same core
      idea, different execution:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Subword tokens, not whole words.</strong> Real models
        split text into subword pieces ("tokens") so they can represent
        any word — including ones never seen in training — as a sequence
        of familiar fragments.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Attention, not a fixed window.</strong> Our toy only ever
        looks at the single previous word. A transformer's attention
        mechanism lets every token look back at every other token in the
        context, weighting each one dynamically — not a hard cutoff.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Learned parameters, not a lookup table.</strong> Our
        predictions come from literally counting word pairs. Real models
        encode patterns across billions of parameters learned from
        training — there's no table to point at, but the underlying
        question being answered at every step is the same one this toy
        answers: <em>given everything so far, what comes next?</em>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DeepDive>

  <WhatILearned>
    Fluent text is not the same thing as correct text — they come from the
    same prediction mechanism, so fluency tells you nothing about accuracy.
    Treat an unverified claim from an LLM the way you'd treat one from a
    stranger: plausible until checked, not true because it sounded sure.
  </WhatILearned>

  <Sources
    sources={[
      {
        title: 'Attention Is All You Need',
        url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762',
        note: 'The 2017 paper that introduced the transformer architecture nearly every modern LLM is built on.',
      },
      {
        title: 'OpenAI Tokenizer',
        url: 'https://platform.openai.com/tokenizer',
        note: 'Paste real text and see exactly how a real model breaks it into tokens.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Large language model — Wikipedia',
        url: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model',
        note: 'A broader overview of how these models are trained and scaled, if you want the fuller picture.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import { scrambleText as decode, prefersReducedMotion } from '../lib/decode';

  const CORPUS = `The cat sat on the mat. The cat likes to nap in the sun. The dog ran across the yard.
  The dog likes to bark at the mailman. The cat and the dog are friends. The cat sat by the window
  and watched the birds. The dog sat by the door and waited for the mail. The sun was warm and the
  cat felt sleepy. The yard was green and the dog loved to run. The mailman came to the door every
  day. The cat did not like the mailman. The dog did not like the vacuum. The vacuum was loud and
  scared the dog. The cat hid under the bed when the vacuum came out. Every morning the cat ate
  breakfast by the window. Every morning the dog waited by the door for a walk. The walk was the
  best part of the dog's day. The nap was the best part of the cat's day. The birds sang in the tree
  by the window. The tree was tall and full of leaves. In the evening the family sat by the fire.
  The cat curled up on the warm rug. The dog lay down next to the cat. They were tired after a long
  day. The stars came out and the house grew quiet. The cat closed its eyes and began to dream. The
  dog let out a big yawn and fell asleep too.`;

  const words = CORPUS.toLowerCase().replace(/[.,!?']/g, '').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
  const bigram = new Map<string, Map<string, number>>();
  for (let i = 0; i < words.length - 1; i++) {
    const ctx = words[i];
    const next = words[i + 1];
    if (!bigram.has(ctx)) bigram.set(ctx, new Map());
    const m = bigram.get(ctx)!;
    m.set(next, (m.get(next) ?? 0) + 1);
  }

  document.getElementById('stat-total')!.textContent = String(words.length);
  document.getElementById('stat-vocab')!.textContent = String(new Set(words).size);
  document.getElementById('stat-contexts')!.textContent = String(bigram.size);

  interface Candidate { word: string; count: number; pct: number; }

  function predict(context: string, topN = 5): Candidate[] | null {
    const lastWord = context.trim().toLowerCase().split(/\s+/).pop();
    if (!lastWord) return null;
    const m = bigram.get(lastWord);
    if (!m) return null;
    const total = Array.from(m.values()).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
    return Array.from(m.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, topN)
      .map(([word, count]) => ({ word, count, pct: (count / total) * 100 }));
  }

  // A generator so the UI can reveal each word as it's actually sampled,
  // not as a typewriter effect played over text computed all at once.
  function* generateSteps(seed: string, numWords = 15): Generator<string> {
    const seq = seed.trim().toLowerCase().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
    yield seq.join(' ');
    for (let i = 0; i < numWords; i++) {
      const last = seq[seq.length - 1];
      const m = bigram.get(last);
      if (!m) return;
      const total = Array.from(m.values()).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
      let r = Math.random() * total;
      let chosen: string | undefined;
      for (const [w, c] of m) {
        r -= c;
        if (r <= 0) { chosen = w; break; }
      }
      seq.push(chosen ?? Array.from(m.keys())[0]);
      yield seq.join(' ');
    }
  }

  const input = document.getElementById('seed-input') as HTMLInputElement;
  const body = document.getElementById('predict-body')!;

  function updatePredictions() {
    const candidates = predict(input.value);
    body.innerHTML = '';
    if (!candidates) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      const td = document.createElement('td');
      td.colSpan = 3;
      td.textContent = 'No match — this word never appeared in the toy training text.';
      td.className = 'predict-empty';
      tr.append(td);
      body.append(tr);
      return;
    }
    const fills: { el: HTMLElement; pct: number }[] = [];
    for (const c of candidates) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      const wordTd = document.createElement('td');
      wordTd.textContent = c.word;
      wordTd.className = 'predict-word';
      const countTd = document.createElement('td');
      countTd.textContent = `${c.count}×`;
      const barTd = document.createElement('td');
      barTd.className = 'predict-bar-cell';
      const track = document.createElement('div');
      track.className = 'bar-track';
      const fill = document.createElement('div');
      fill.className = 'bar-fill';
      fill.style.width = '0%';
      track.append(fill);
      const pctLabel = document.createElement('span');
      pctLabel.className = 'predict-pct';
      pctLabel.textContent = `${c.pct.toFixed(1)}%`;
      barTd.append(track, pctLabel);
      tr.append(wordTd, countTd, barTd);
      body.append(tr);
      fills.push({ el: fill, pct: c.pct });
    }
    // Bars start at 0% (painted above) and only get their real width next
    // frame, so the CSS transition actually has something to animate from
    // instead of snapping straight to the target on first paint.
    requestAnimationFrame(() => requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      for (const f of fills) f.el.style.width = `${f.pct}%`;
    }));
  }

  input.addEventListener('input', updatePredictions);
  updatePredictions();

  let generateTimer: number | undefined;

  // Decode effect: a freshly-sampled word cycles through noise glyphs
  // before locking to its real value, like a terminal resolving a signal.
  // Core animation lives in src/lib/decode.ts; this page just owns the
  // 'scramble' glow class around it.
  const WORD_PAUSE_MS = 100;

  function scrambleWord(span: HTMLElement, finalWord: string, onDone: () => void) {
    decode(span, finalWord, {
      frames: 8,
      frameMs: 40,
      onDone: () => { span.classList.remove('scramble'); onDone(); },
    });
  }

  document.getElementById('generate-btn')!.addEventListener('click', () => {
    if (generateTimer) { clearTimeout(generateTimer); generateTimer = undefined; }
    const seed = input.value.trim() || 'the cat';
    const outEl = document.getElementById('generated-output')!;
    outEl.textContent = '';
    const steps = generateSteps(seed, 15);

    if (prefersReducedMotion()) {
      let last = '';
      for (const value of steps) last = value;
      outEl.textContent = last;
      return;
    }

    const first = steps.next();
    if (first.done) return;
    outEl.textContent = first.value; // the seed itself, shown plainly

    const revealNext = () => {
      const { value, done } = steps.next();
      if (done) return;
      const newWord = value.split(' ').pop()!;
      outEl.append(' ');
      const span = document.createElement('span');
      span.className = 'scramble';
      outEl.append(span);
      scrambleWord(span, newWord, () => {
        generateTimer = window.setTimeout(revealNext, WORD_PAUSE_MS);
      });
    };
    generateTimer = window.setTimeout(revealNext, WORD_PAUSE_MS);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .lab { margin: var(--sp-6) 0; padding: var(--sp-4); border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--bg-1); }
  .section-heading { margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  .lab-note { color: var(--text-2); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); }

  .corpus-stats {
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line); border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .cs-cell { background: var(--bg-0); padding: var(--sp-2); display: grid; gap: 2px; text-align: center; }
  .cs-key { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.65rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--text-2); text-transform: uppercase; }
  .cs-val { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step-0); font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) { .corpus-stats { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }

  .pw-label { display: block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--text-1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  #seed-input {
    width: 100%; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step-1); padding: var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); color: var(--text-0);
  }
  #seed-input:focus { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

  .table-scroll { margin-top: var(--sp-4); overflow-x: auto; }
  .predict-table { width: 100%; min-width: 28rem; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .predict-table th { text-align: left; color: var(--text-2); font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) var(--sp-1); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-bright); }
  .predict-table td { padding: var(--sp-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--text-1); }
  .predict-word { color: var(--text-0); font-weight: 700; }
  .predict-empty { color: var(--text-2); font-style: italic; }
  .predict-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  .predict-bar-cell { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--sp-3); min-width: 9rem; }
  .bar-track { flex: 1; height: 0.8em; background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); overflow: hidden; position: relative; }
  .bar-fill {
    height: 100%; background: var(--accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--accent), 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 50%, transparent);
    transition: width 500ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
    position: relative; overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* A scan band sweeping across every filled bar — reads as live telemetry, not a static gauge. */
  .bar-fill::after {
    content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 200%;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 0%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-0) 60%, transparent) 50%, transparent 100%);
    animation: scan 1.6s linear infinite;
  }
  @keyframes scan { from { transform: translateX(-50%); } to { transform: translateX(0%); } }
  .predict-pct { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; white-space: nowrap; text-shadow: 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 60%, transparent); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .bar-fill { transition: none; }
    .bar-fill::after { animation: none; }
  }

  .generate-row { margin-top: var(--sp-4); }
  .ex-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); color: var(--text-1); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .ex-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--text-0); }
  .generated-output { margin-top: var(--sp-3); font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--text-0); min-height: 1.6em; letter-spacing: 0.02em; }
  .generated-output :global(.scramble) { color: var(--accent); text-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--accent), 0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 60%, transparent); }
</style>
